course: dictionary
lesson: 4744
title: Horsehair
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Horsehair is the hair from a horse's mane or tail. You can work out its meaning by splitting it into two smaller words: horse and hair.
explain: Read the context: «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings and makes them vibrate; traditional bow hair is horsehair.» The clues are bow, strings and hair, so horsehair is the hair on a bow.
explain: Ask yourself what word would fit. Put "hair from a horse" in the sentence in place of horsehair. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Horsehair is a noun that we usually do not count, so we say some horsehair.
fact: The bows of violins, violas and cellos are strung with horsehair.
fact: Rosin is rubbed on the horsehair so that it grips the strings.
fact: In the past, horsehair was used to stuff mattresses and sofas.
fact: In the UK, judges and barristers have worn wigs made of horsehair.
exercise: «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings.» What is horsehair on a bow? | Steel strings from a guitar | Pine wood from a tree | *The hair from a horse's mane or tail | Resin from a tin
exercise: Which instrument has a bow strung with horsehair? | A piano | A flute | A drum | *A violin
exercise: Look at the word horsehair. Which two smaller words make it up? | hose and hair | *horse and hair | horse and air | house and hare
test: In old houses, what was horsehair used for? | Bricks for the chimney | Glass for the windows | Paint for the doors | *Horsehair plaster on the walls
test: Which people in the UK have worn wigs made of horsehair? | Nurses | Football goalkeepers | Pilots | *Judges and barristers
exam: Ben reads «Horsehair was used to stuff old sofas.» What does stuff mean here? | To paint | To cut into small pieces | To tidy away | *To fill with soft material
